.br
.B crossmacro
\fBscreen pixel|wait-color|search-color|search-image|wait-image|image-click\fR ... [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-log-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.br
.B crossmacro
\fBscreenshot\fR ((\fB\-\-output\fR|\fB\-o\fR) \fIPATH\fR|\fB\-\-clipboard\fR) [\fB\-\-region\fR \fIX\fR \fIY\fR \fIWIDTH\fR \fIHEIGHT\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-log-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.br
.B crossmacro
\fBheadless\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-log-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.br
.B crossmacro
\fB\-\-headless\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-log-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.br
.B crossmacro
\fBmcp\fR [\fB\-\-restricted\fR] [\fB\-\-log-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
CrossMacro provides GUI and CLI workflows for macro playback, validation, recording,
environment diagnostics, and background headless runtime.
If no CLI command is provided, CrossMacro starts GUI mode.
.PP
On Linux, CrossMacro can run in two different input modes.
The preferred packaged path is daemon-backed mode, where the app talks to
\fBcrossmacro.service\fR over \fI/run/crossmacro/crossmacro.sock\fR and the daemon
service user owns device access.
The desktop user must be a member of the \fBcrossmacro\fR group to use that socket.
After adding a user to the group, log out and back in or reboot before starting
CrossMacro again, because existing sessions keep their old group list.
Directory identities resolved through NSS, including AD, LDAP, and SSSD, are
supported. Use \fBgpasswd -a <identity> crossmacro\fR to add a local group membership
without modifying the directory service.
Some channels and environments intentionally support direct device mode instead,
where the app process itself needs access to \fI/dev/uinput\fR and, for recording or hotkeys,
readable \fI/dev/input/event*\fR devices.
Direct device readiness does not fix daemon socket permission failures.
On X11, CrossMacro tries native X11 capture and playback first; a supported native X11
session does not require daemon-backed or direct device fallback.
Flatpak and AppImage use direct device mode on Wayland and do not probe the host daemon.
The required direct device permissions can be granted by Quick Setup.
Arch and CachyOS package hooks provision the \fBcrossmacro\fR sysusers entry before service
start or restart, and print manual remediation when they cannot confirm adding the
installing user to the \fBcrossmacro\fR group automatically.
Wayfire IPC discovery checks \fBWAYFIRE_SOCKET\fR, then \fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR, then
temp-directory socket candidates.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show command help.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show version. Development builds include the short source revision for bug reports.
.TP
.B \-\-start-minimized
Start GUI minimized. When tray support is available, CrossMacro starts hidden to tray instead.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print command output as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-log-level \fILEVEL\fR
Override logging level for the current command.
Supported values: Verbose, Debug, Information, Warning, Error, Fatal.
.SH HELP AND ROUTING POLICY
.TP
.B crossmacro \-\-help
Shows top-level help.
.TP
.B crossmacro <command> \-\-help
Shows help for that command topic (for example \fBrun\fR or \fBrecord\fR).
.TP
.B Standalone command options
Standalone CLI-only options such as \fB\-\-json\fR and \fB\-\-log-level\fR require a command and return exit code 2.
.TP
.B Non-CLI startup switches
Known GUI startup switches are routed to GUI startup (for example \fB--start-minimized\fR, \fB--drm\fR, \fB--fbdev\fR, \fB--display\fR, Finder-style \fB-psn_*\fR).
.TP
.B Unknown tokens
Unknown commands/options return CLI parse error (exit code 2) instead of falling through to GUI.
.SH OPTION ALIASES
.TP
.B record \-\-output, \-o
Both forms are accepted and required for output path selection.
.TP
.B headless, \-\-headless
Both forms start headless runtime mode.
.TP
.B mcp
Starts a local stdio Model Context Protocol server. Standard output is reserved
for MCP JSON-RPC messages; diagnostics are written to standard error and normal
log files. MCP does not acquire the GUI/headless single-instance guard, so it can
run alongside either runtime. It stops when its client closes standard input or
interrupts the process.
.SH VALUE RULES
.TP
.B play
\fB\-\-repeat\fR, \fB\-\-repeat-delay-ms\fR, \fB\-\-countdown\fR, and \fB\-\-timeout\fR must be >= 0.
Providing \fB\-\-loop\fR without \fB\-\-repeat\fR means infinite loop mode.
Providing \fB\-\-repeat\fR with value > 0 implies loop semantics for that playback run.
\fB\-\-repeat 0\fR requires \fB\-\-loop\fR (infinite mode).
.TP
.B run
\fB\-\-speed\fR must be between 0.1 and 10.0.
\fB\-\-countdown\fR and \fB\-\-timeout\fR must be >= 0.
\fB\-\-asset NAME PNG_PATH\fR is repeatable; NAME must match
\fB[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\fR and PNG_PATH must be a valid native PNG.
.TP
.B window and screen
\fBwindow wait --timeout-ms\fR must be >= 0. \fBscreen search-color --tolerance\fR must be between 0 and 255.
\fBscreen search-color\fR bounds are end-exclusive and must produce a positive region.
The repeated screen commands use a fixed 50 millisecond cadence and default to
a total 5000 millisecond timeout. One-frame pixel and image searches keep an
internal five-second capture safety deadline.
Image command timeouts and regions must be non-negative and positive-sized respectively.
Image similarity must be finite and between 0.0 and 1.0.
.TP
.B screenshot
At least one destination, \fB\-\-output\fR/\fB\-o\fR or \fB\-\-clipboard\fR, is required.
\fB\-\-region\fR width and height must be positive.
.TP
.B profile
\fBprofile delete\fR requires \fB\-\-force\fR. Profile ids and display names are resolved case-insensitively.
.TP
.B text-expansion
\fBadd\fR rejects duplicate triggers. \fBtest\fR only reports a matching expansion and never types or pastes into the desktop.
.TP
.B record
\fB\-\-duration\fR must be >= 0, and at least one of \fB\-\-mouse\fR or \fB\-\-keyboard\fR must be true.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B play
Load and play a macro file without starting GUI.
.TP
.B macro validate
Validate macro syntax and playback compatibility.
.TP
.B macro info
Show macro metadata and event breakdown.
.TP
.B doctor
Run environment and input capability diagnostics. On Linux, daemon-backed checks and direct
device checks are reported separately. With \fB--json\fR, inspect daemon-specific details such as
socket status, required group, current process groups, session membership, and remediation.
Direct input readiness can pass while daemon IPC still warns or fails.
.TP
.B setup
Grant temporary direct input access for the current Flatpak or AppImage Wayland session without
starting the GUI. Flatpak uses \fBflatpak-spawn\fR and the host's usable setuid \fBpkexec\fR
or \fBrun0\fR fallback; AppImage uses the same authorization selection directly. The alias
\fBquick-setup\fR is accepted. GUI-launched setup requires a host graphical polkit authentication
agent; terminal \fBpkexec\fR setup can use its textual authentication prompt. Returns environment
error code 5 when the current session is not applicable.
.TP
.B settings get|set|list-keys|reset
Read, update, list, or reset scriptable settings keys.
Supported UI/update keys include \fBui.theme\fR, \fBui.language\fR, \fBui.trayIcon\fR,
\fBui.startMinimized\fR, and \fBupdates.checkForUpdates\fR. The portal restore token
key \fBscreen.portalRestoreToken\fR is status/reset only; raw token values are never printed.
.TP
.B profile
Manage GUI profiles from scripts.
.PP
Profile syntax:
.br
\fBprofile list\fR
.br
\fBprofile current\fR
.br
\fBprofile create <name>\fR
.br
\fBprofile switch <name-or-id>\fR
.br
\fBprofile rename <name-or-id> <new-name>\fR
.br
\fBprofile delete <name-or-id> --force\fR
.PP
Archive \fBprofile export/import\fR is intentionally deferred until portable backup and restore semantics are specified.
.TP
.B text-expansion
Manage stored text expansion entries.
.PP
Text expansion syntax:
.br
\fBtext-expansion list [--profile <name-or-id>]\fR
.br
\fBtext-expansion add <trigger> <replacement> [--method CtrlV|CtrlShiftV|ShiftInsert] [--insertion-mode Paste|DirectTyping] [--direct-typing-method FastBatch|CompatibleKeyByKey] [--profile <name-or-id>]\fR
.br
\fBtext-expansion remove|enable|disable|test <trigger> [--profile <name-or-id>]\fR
.PP
\fB--profile\fR temporarily loads that profile's text-expansion storage for the operation and restores the active profile storage afterward; it does not switch the active profile. \fBtext\fR is accepted as a short alias for \fBtext-expansion\fR.
.TP
.B schedule
Manage scheduled macro tasks in the active profile.
.PP
Schedule syntax:
.br
\fBschedule list\fR
.br
\fBschedule run <task-id>\fR
.br
\fBschedule add --name <name> --macro <path> [--interval <duration>|--at <datetime>|--weekly <days> --time <HH:mm>] [--speed <value>] [--enabled <bool>]\fR
.br
\fBschedule edit <task-id> [--name <name>] [--macro <path>] [--interval <duration>|--at <datetime>|--weekly <days> --time <HH:mm>] [--speed <value>] [--enabled <bool>]\fR
.br
\fBschedule remove|enable|disable|next <task-id>\fR
.TP
.B shortcut
Manage shortcut-bound macro tasks in the active profile.
.PP
Shortcut syntax:
.br
\fBshortcut list\fR
.br
\fBshortcut run <task-id>\fR
.br
\fBshortcut add --name <name> --macro <path> --hotkey <keys> [--speed <value>] [--loop] [--repeat <n>] [--repeat-delay-ms <ms>] [--random-repeat-delay <min-ms> <max-ms>] [--run-while-held] [--window-rule <field> <match-mode> <value>] [--enabled <bool>]\fR
.br
\fBshortcut edit <task-id> [--name <name>] [--macro <path>] [--hotkey <keys>] [--speed <value>] [--loop] [--repeat <n>] [--repeat-delay-ms <ms>] [--random-repeat-delay <min-ms> <max-ms>] [--run-while-held] [--window-rule <field> <match-mode> <value>] [--clear-window-rules] [--enabled <bool>]\fR
.br
\fBshortcut remove|enable|disable <task-id>\fR
.br
\fBshortcut bind <task-id> <hotkey>\fR
.PP
Repeat \fB--window-rule <field> <match-mode> <value>\fR to limit a hotkey to
focused windows. Fields are \fBWindowClass\fR, \fBWindowTitle\fR, and
\fBProcessName\fR; match modes are \fBEquals\fR, \fBContains\fR, and
\fBRegex\fR. Rules use OR semantics. Providing rules with \fBshortcut edit\fR
replaces all existing rules; \fB--clear-window-rules\fR restores global behavior.
Explicit \fBshortcut run\fR execution ignores window rules.
.TP
.B record
Record input events and save a macro file.
.TP
.B run
Run inline event steps directly from CLI (without a .macro file).
Supported steps:
\fBmove abs|rel|rel-logical|rel-raw\fR, \fBdown\fR, \fBup\fR, \fBclick\fR, \fBscroll\fR,
\fBkey down|up\fR, \fBtap\fR, \fBdelay\fR, \fBtype\fR,
\fBmouse position <x_variable> <y_variable>\fR,
\fBset\fR variables, \fBinc\fR/\fBdec\fR/\fBmul\fR/\fBdiv\fR counters,
\fBshell "<command>" [retries] [backoff_ms] [timeout_ms]\fR,
\fBshell capture "<command>" exit_var stdout_var stderr_var [retries] [backoff_ms] [timeout_ms]\fR,
\fBshell input "<stdin text>" "<command>" [retries] [backoff_ms] [timeout_ms]\fR,
\fBshell capture-input "<stdin text>" "<command>" exit_var stdout_var stderr_var [retries] [backoff_ms] [timeout_ms]\fR,
\fBrepeat\fR blocks, \fBif\fR/\fBelse\fR, \fBwhile\fR, and \fBfor\fR blocks,
screen-reading steps \fBpixelcolor\fR, \fBwaitcolor\fR, \fBpixelsearch\fR,
\fBimagesearch\fR, \fBimageclick\fR, and \fBwaitimage\fR,
plus loop control with \fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR.
Named image assets are supplied with repeatable
\fB\-\-asset NAME PNG_PATH\fR options and are referenced by image-step name.
\fBpixelcolor\fR and \fBimagesearch\fR inspect one frame. \fBwaitcolor\fR,
\fBpixelsearch\fR, \fBimageclick\fR, and \fBwaitimage\fR retry every 50
milliseconds for a total five-second default budget.
Randomized timing is supported with
\fBdelay random <min> <max>\fR or \fBdelay random <min>..<max>\fR.
.PP
Shell steps run through the platform shell (\fB/bin/sh -c\fR on Unix,
\fBcmd.exe /S /C\fR on Windows). \fBretries\fR is the number of extra attempts
after the first; \fBbackoff_ms\fR is the delay before retrying; \fBtimeout_ms\fR
is per attempt and \fB0\fR means no timeout. Variables inside command and stdin
payloads are resolved before execution. Quote command payloads when using numeric
options so command arguments are not confused with retry/backoff/timeout values.
Capture forms write exit code, stdout, and stderr variables; use \fB_\fR
to ignore a captured value. Normal and input forms fail on final non-zero exits,
while capture forms continue and let scripts branch on the captured exit code.
Captured stdout/stderr are capped at 65536 characters per stream. Shell steps
execute arbitrary commands, so only run trusted macros. Flatpak builds execute
them in a stricter nested sandbox without inheriting the parent application's host
files, devices, D-Bus permissions, portals, or host command channel. Native and
AppImage builds use the normal platform shell. Use \fB$$NAME\fR when the shell
should receive \fB$NAME\fR literally instead of resolving a CrossMacro variable.
.PP
Runtime primitive steps mirror the first-class \fBclipboard\fR, \fBwindow\fR,
and \fBscreen\fR command families. Use \fBclipboard get <var>\fR and
\fBclipboard set <text>\fR for text clipboard state. \fBclipboard capture ctrl+c <var>\fR
or \fBclipboard capture ctrl+shift+c <var>\fR sends the selected copy shortcut,
waits a fixed 10 milliseconds for clipboard ownership to update, and stores the
clipboard text in the named runtime variable. Select text in the target application
before the capture step. Use \fBwindow ...\fR forms for
active/search/wait/focus/close/move/resize/workspace operations, and
\fBpixelcolor\fR, \fBwaitcolor\fR, or \fBpixelsearch\fR for screen color reads.
Use \fBmouse position <x_variable> <y_variable>\fR to store one live global
cursor sample as signed integer runtime variables. The destination names must
differ. It does not create a separate background cursor; later move and click
steps still control the user's pointer. The step fails when the session cannot
provide a global cursor position.
Use \fBimagesearch\fR, \fBimageclick\fR, or \fBwaitimage\fR for native PNG
template matching. Similarity defaults to 0.95, automatic matching is the
default, and
\fBimageclick\fR defaults to the left button; right and middle are optional.
Image assets are native 8-bit PNG files only, not JPG files.
Target colors use six-digit \fBRRGGBB\fR values or \fB$var\fR references to
values written by \fBpixelcolor\fR. Unsupported platforms return runtime errors.
.SS Direct input commands
The one-step input commands can be used without a \fBrun\fR wrapper:
\fBmove\fR, \fBclick\fR, \fBdown\fR, \fBup\fR, \fBscroll\fR,
\fBkey\fR, \fBtap\fR, \fBtype\fR, and \fBdelay\fR.
They use the same compiler, coordinate spaces, button/key names, and runtime
preflight as \fBrun\fR. Use \fBrun\fR for variables, conditions, loops, or
multiple steps.
.TP
.B move, click, down, up, scroll, key, tap, type, delay
Execute one common input primitive without a \fBrun\fR wrapper. These aliases
use the same input compiler and coordinate spaces as \fBrun\fR and accept
\fB\-\-dry-run\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR, and \fB\-\-log-level\fR. Use \fBrun\fR for
variables, conditions, loops, screen/window steps, or multiple operations.
.PP
Examples:
.br
\fBcrossmacro move abs 200 200\fR
.br
\fBcrossmacro click left\fR
.br
\fBcrossmacro key down CTRL\fR
.br
\fBcrossmacro type "hello world"\fR
.br
\fBcrossmacro delay 50\fR
.TP
.B clipboard
Read or replace text clipboard contents without writing a run script.
Text mode for \fBclipboard get\fR prints only the clipboard value; JSON mode includes the value in \fBdata.value\fR.
\fBclipboard clear\fR replaces clipboard text with an empty value.
Unsupported platforms return a non-zero environment error.
.TP
.B window
Query and mutate desktop windows without writing a run script.
Supported forms include \fBactive\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBsearch (--title <text>|--class <text>)\fR,
\fBwait (--title <text>|--class <text>) [--timeout-ms <n>]\fR,
\fBfocus (--address <id>|--title <text>|--class <text>)\fR,
\fBclose (--address <id>|--title <text>)\fR, \fBmove --active <x> <y>\fR,
\fBresize --active <width> <height>\fR, \fBcenter|maximize|fullscreen|float --active\fR,
and workspace \fBget\fR, \fBswitch <name>\fR, \fBmove-active <name>\fR, and
\fBmove-window --address <id> <name>\fR.
.PP
Title and class selectors use case-insensitive substring matching. Unsupported window managers return a non-zero environment error.
.TP
.B screen
Read screen pixels or match native PNG templates without writing a run script.
Supported forms include \fBpixel <x> <y>\fR,
\fBpixel --relative <dx> <dy>\fR,
\fBwait-color <x> <y> <RRGGBB> [--timeout-ms <n>]\fR, and
\fBsearch-color <x1> <y1> <x2> <y2> <RRGGBB> [--timeout-ms <n>] [--tolerance <0..255>]\fR.
\fBsearch-image <image-path> [--region <x> <y> <width> <height>] [--similarity <0..1>] [--matchmode <auto|first|best>]\fR
inspects one frame. \fBwait-image\fR and
\fBimage-click <image-path> [--timeout-ms <n>] [--button <left|right|middle>] [--region <x> <y> <width> <height>] [--similarity <0..1>] [--matchmode <auto|first|best>]\fR
retry for 5000 milliseconds by default. Image regions are end-exclusive.
\fB\-\-matchmode auto\fR is the default with 0.95 confidence; wait-image and
image-click require two consistent frames before success.
\fB\-\-matchmode first\fR and \fB\-\-matchmode best\fR select advanced
deterministic modes; automatic matching remains the default.
When a Wayland capture provider is available, a region may be stitched from
intersecting monitor outputs. Gaps outside real outputs are voids and are
ignored by matching; image-click will not click a template that crosses one.
.PP
JSON output includes coordinates, color metadata, provider name, and search/wait result metadata.
.TP
.B screenshot
Capture the current screen frame to a PNG file.
.PP
Screenshot syntax:
.br
\fBscreenshot --output ./shot.png\fR
.br
\fBscreenshot -o ./shot.png --json\fR
.br
\fBscreenshot --clipboard\fR
.br
\fBscreenshot --output ./shot.png --clipboard\fR
.br
\fBscreenshot --output ./crop.png --region 100 100 800 600\fR
.PP
Screenshot capture uses the same platform frame providers as screen pixel reads.
Unsupported platforms or sessions return a non-zero environment error.
.TP
.B headless
Run background services without GUI (hotkeys, scheduler, shortcuts, text expansion)
until interrupted.
Recording hotkey toggles capture start/stop.
Playback hotkey toggles play/stop for the last macro recorded in the same headless session.
Pause hotkey toggles pause/resume for active playback.
.TP
.B mcp
Run CrossMacro as a local stdio Model Context Protocol server for an MCP-capable
client. Standard output is reserved for MCP JSON-RPC messages; diagnostics use
standard error and normal log files. The complete tool, security, and connection
reference is available in the project MCP documentation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or parsing error.
.TP
.B 3
File access/read error.
.TP
.B 4
Macro validation error.
.TP
.B 5
Environment/permission/runtime readiness error.
.TP
.B 6
Runtime playback or capture error.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by signal (for example Ctrl+C).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B crossmacro play ./demo.macro --speed 1.25 --repeat 3
Play a macro file three times.
.TP
.B crossmacro doctor --json
Run diagnostics and print JSON.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --step "move abs 800 400" --step "click left" --dry-run
Compile and validate direct run steps without sending input.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --step "move abs 800 400" --step "click current left"
Force click at live cursor even after absolute moves.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --step "mouse position mouse_x mouse_y" --step "move abs 256 512" --step "click left" --step "move abs $mouse_x $mouse_y"
Save the cursor position, click a fixed point, then restore the saved position.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --step 'shell "echo done" 1 250 5000'
Run a shell command with one retry, 250 ms backoff, and a 5000 ms per-attempt timeout.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --step 'shell capture "printf ok" code out err'
Run a shell command and store exit code, stdout, and stderr in runtime variables.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --step 'shell capture-input "hello" "cat" code out err'
Pipe stdin to a shell command and store exit code, stdout, and stderr in runtime variables.
.TP
.B crossmacro run --file ./steps.txt
Load run steps from a plain text file (one step per line, '#' comments allowed).
.TP
.B crossmacro clipboard get --json
Read clipboard text and print structured JSON.
.TP
.B crossmacro headless
Start headless runtime and keep running until interrupted.
.TP
.B crossmacro mcp
Start the local stdio MCP server for a configured MCP client.
.TP
.B crossmacro \-\-start-minimized
Start GUI hidden to tray when tray icon support is available for the current session.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project repository: https://github.com/alper-han/CrossMacro
